{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>These are the journeys, etc.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 12:3) said: every place where ״אֵלֶּה״ is said it invalidates the preceding ones. But in the matter before us we do not see that there preceded this statement any other journeys to imply that the verse intended to invalidate them. It might seem that it intended to invalidate all journeys other than these that exist in the world, even though they were not mentioned. But it appeared to me that the journeys were not for praise — and would that they had not existed in the world, for most of them were punishments for the sin of the spies. Also the earlier ones were on a distant route, so that they would not reconsider and return, as it is written (Exodus 13:17): ״וְלֹא נָחָם אֱלֹהִים דֶּרֶךְ אֶרֶץ פְּלִשְׁתִּים״. From this you learn that they were not according to the desired plan and the proper schedule for the festival seasons. Moreover, our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Yalkut Yitro 293), and this is their language: ״וְכָל נְתִיבוֹתֶיהָ שָׁלוֹם״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, sought to give the Torah to Israel when they came out of Egypt, and they would quarrel with one another, etc.; what is written? ״וַיִּסְעוּ מִסֻּכּוֹת וַיַּחֲנוּ בְאֵיתָם״ — they would journey in strife and encamp in strife, etc.; end quote. And according to this explanation there is nothing of precious value in these journeys that would warrant invalidating any others." "<b>And it is possible</b> that since in these journeys mentioned in the parashah there are two levels: first, the journeys that took place in the first year before the decree of the spies; second, the journeys they made because of the decree of the spies, which were not in the mind of HaShem to do anything other than what they did for the two years — and these latter journeys are inferior in status to those before them, and corresponding to them HaShem intended the word אֵלֶּה to invalidate them. And this is the verse's measure: <b>אֵלֶּה מַסְעֵי</b> etc. — meaning, those mentioned immediately below are the proper and necessary journeys, and all others are invalid, for they were nothing but chastisements to move them about in the wilderness through reproofs for the sin of the spies." "<b>Further,</b> it appears worthwhile to raise the question of why the verse chose to enumerate the journeys and not the encampments — all the more so according to their words, may their memory be for blessing (Midrash Rabbah here), for they said: a parable of a king, etc. — here they camped, here they traveled, etc.; end quote — for this is more about remembering the encampments, not the journeys. Also according to another midrash (Tanchuma) that said He mentioned the journeys to establish reward for them for the World to Come, as it is written (Isaiah 35:1) ״יְשֻׂשׂוּם מִדְבָּר״ etc. — according to this it would have been fitting to enumerate the encampments." "<b>However,</b> it may be explained according to the words of the men of truth (Zohar vol. 2, 157a), who said that the journeying of Israel through the wilderness was to sift the sparks of holiness that the man of Belial, who encamps in the desolate wilderness, had seized by force — for there he established his place, the place of the fiery serpent and scorpion, and the congregation of HaShem passed through there to extract what he had swallowed from his mouth. And this is the reason Israel would encamp in one place for a year and in another for twelve hours — namely, according to what was needed for the sifting of the sparks present in that place. And this sifting has no force in all the world that can accomplish it other than complete holiness and in the sod of the perfection of the bond of the collective and the bond of the individual: the complete holiness is the Shekhinah and Israel and the Torah, and the bond of the individual is the sixty myriad holy souls, and the bond of the collective is Moses our teacher in the sod of ״מֹשֶׁה עַמּוֹ״ (Isaiah 63:11), for he is the tree from which spark the souls of the sixty myriad who were there, and they were empowered by divine aid through the indwelling of the One who dwells in the dwelling, and they shattered the teeth of the wicked and performed a mighty sifting." "<b>Accordingly,</b> the ultimate purpose of the action was at the time of journeying — for when they journeyed, everything resembling the category of holiness journeyed with them, not at the time of encamping, when nothing had yet been accomplished with it. And this is what is alluded to in the statement <b>אֵלֶּה מַסְעֵי</b> — אֵלֶּה invalidates all the journeys in the world, for there is no journey like this one, in which holy and precious treasure beyond compare journeyed with them. And even though the Patriarchs preceded and traversed places and did their part in sifting sparks, they did not reach this threshold. And the verse itself states the reason for the superiority of these journeys — because they are those of the children of Israel <b>who came out of the land of Egypt</b> and were refined in the iron crucible, which is the land of Egypt as stated, and through this their souls became worthy to sift the sparks of holiness in every place to which they would come there. And further, <b>לְצִבְאוֹתָם</b> — which is the perfection of the host upon which the Shekhinah rests, namely the number of sixty myriad, and fewer than this number does not constitute that perfection." "<b>And you will find</b> that they said, may their memory be for blessing (Genesis Rabbah 71; Mekhilta Yitro), that for this reason HaShem did not give the Torah to the earlier generations, because the completeness of the host upon which the Shekhinah — called Tzevaot — would rest was not among them. Also, in the word <b>le-tziv'otam</b> He alluded to the indwelling of the Shekhinah with them. Behold, before you there is a record of a holy people, a record of the complete number, and the indwelling of the Shekhinah; and corresponding to the unifying conjunction of the totality of the complete number, He said ״בְּיַד מֹשֶׁה וְאַהֲרֹן״ — Moses is the unifying conjunction of the complete number, and Aaron is the attendant of the Shekhinah who brings near and unifies to it all the branches, as is known in the sod of the sacrifice." "<b>אֲשֶׁר יָצְאוּ מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם לְצִבְאֹתָם</b>. According to what we explained, that the word ״אֵלֶּה״ announced the select journeys and excluded the inferior journeys, the verse juxtaposed to the statement ״אֵלֶּה״ the statement <b>אֲשֶׁר יָצְאוּ</b> etc. <b>לְצִבְאֹתָם</b>, to alert you that the select ones are those that preceded the spies — they are the children of Israel who came out of the land of Egypt בְּצִבְאוֹתָם. That is, the hosts themselves, which came out, to exclude the journeys that were from the time of the spies, which were not לְצִבְאוֹתָם, for a decree had been issued for them to die in the wilderness, and from that year itself a portion of them had already died and the hosts that had come out of Egypt were no longer in existence. And His saying <b>בְּיַד מֹשֶׁה וְאַהֲרֹן</b> means that the journeys were in their hand, as it is written in parashat BeHa'alotekha (Numbers 9:20): ״וְעַל פִּי ה׳ יִסָּעוּ״ etc. ״עַל פִּי ה׳ בְּיַד מֹשֶׁה״, and He joined Aaron with him, for Aaron is the one who blows the trumpets for their journeying; therefore he was partnered with Moses."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:17:28.294708034Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}